+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">Text Generation with GPT-2</h3>'

+ '<p class="l-text">GPT-2 is a <strong>decoder-only transformer</strong> trained on 40 GB of Internet text with a single objective: predict the next token given all previous tokens. That simple objective, applied at massive scale, produces a model that can continue any prompt in a stylistically consistent, grammatically fluent, and often surprisingly coherent way. Understanding how to load it, invoke it, and tune its generation parameters is the foundation for all subsequent work with causal language models.</p>'

+ '<h4 class="l-subsection-title">Loading GPT-2</h4>'

+ '<p class="l-text">GPT-2 is available in four sizes on the Hub. The base model (117M parameters) fits comfortably in CPU RAM for inference and is the standard starting point for fine-tuning experiments on modest hardware.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON (DEMO)</span> Load GPT-2 Model &amp; Tokenizer <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> transformers <span class="kw">import</span> AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\n<span class="kw">import</span> torch\n\n<span class="cm"># Load tokenizer and model</span>\n<span class="cm"># Available sizes: gpt2 | gpt2-medium | gpt2-large | gpt2-xl</span>\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"gpt2"</span>)\nmodel     = AutoModelForCausalLM.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"gpt2"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># GPT-2\'s tokenizer has no pad token by default</span>\n<span class="cm"># Set it to eos_token for batch inference compatibility</span>\ntokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token\n\n<span class="cm"># Move to GPU if available</span>\ndevice = <span class="str">"cuda"</span> <span class="kw">if</span> torch.cuda.<span class="fn">is_available</span>() <span class="kw">else</span> <span class="str">"cpu"</span>\nmodel  = model.<span class="fn">to</span>(device)\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Parameters: {model.num_parameters():,}"</span>)   <span class="cm"># 124,439,808</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Vocabulary: {tokenizer.vocab_size:,}"</span>)       <span class="cm"># 50,257</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Max length: {tokenizer.model_max_length}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># 1024</span></code></pre></div></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">The model uses <strong>Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE)</strong> with a 50 257-token vocabulary. Every possible byte sequence is representable, so GPT-2 never produces an unknown token \u2014 even for arbitrary Unicode, code, or URLs.</p>'

+ '<h4 class="l-subsection-title">Basic Generation</h4>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span> Basic Text Generation <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code>prompt = <span class="str">"The future of machine learning is"</span>\ninputs = <span class="fn">tokenizer</span>(prompt, return_tensors=<span class="str">"pt"</span>).<span class="fn">to</span>(device)\n\n<span class="cm"># generate() returns a tensor of token IDs</span>\noutput_ids = model.<span class="fn">generate</span>(\n    **inputs,\n    max_length=<span class="num">100</span>,\n    pad_token_id=tokenizer.eos_token_id\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># Decode, skipping the prompt tokens</span>\ngenerated = tokenizer.<span class="fn">decode</span>(\n    output_ids[<span class="num">0</span>][inputs[<span class="str">"input_ids"</span>].shape[<span class="num">1</span>]:],\n    skip_special_tokens=<span class="kw">True</span>\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(generated)</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How this minimal generation snippet works:</strong> 1) <code>tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors="pt").to(device)</code> encodes the prompt into PyTorch tensors and moves them to the same device as the model. 2) <code>model.generate(**inputs, max_length=100, pad_token_id=tokenizer.eos_token_id)</code> runs the default autoregressive loop until 100 total tokens are produced; passing <code>pad_token_id</code> avoids the warning GPT-2 emits because it has no native pad token. 3) Slicing <code>output_ids[0][inputs["input_ids"].shape[1]:]</code> drops the prompt tokens so <code>tokenizer.decode(..., skip_special_tokens=True)</code> prints only the freshly generated continuation.</p>'

/* ── generate() Parameters ────────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h4 class="l-subsection-title">generate() Parameters Reference</h4>'

+ '<p class="l-text">The <code>generate()</code> method exposes every knob that controls the decoding process. These parameters fall into three conceptual groups: <em>length controls</em>, <em>quality constraints</em>, and <em>stochastic sampling controls</em>. Understanding each one lets you dial in exactly the behavior your application needs.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span> generate() Parameters Reference <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code>output_ids = model.<span class="fn">generate</span>(\n    input_ids=inputs[<span class="str">"input_ids"</span>],\n    attention_mask=inputs[<span class="str">"attention_mask"</span>],\n\n    <span class="cm"># ── Length controls ──────────────────────────────────────</span>\n    max_length=<span class="num">200</span>,          <span class="cm"># hard cap on total sequence length (prompt + output)</span>\n    max_new_tokens=<span class="num">150</span>,      <span class="cm"># cleaner: cap only newly generated tokens</span>\n    min_length=<span class="num">50</span>,           <span class="cm"># prevent early termination</span>\n    min_new_tokens=<span class="num">30</span>,       <span class="cm"># prevent trivially short outputs</span>\n\n    <span class="cm"># ── Stopping ─────────────────────────────────────────────</span>\n    eos_token_id=tokenizer.eos_token_id,   <span class="cm"># stop on end-of-sequence</span>\n    pad_token_id=tokenizer.eos_token_id,   <span class="cm"># required for batching</span>\n\n    <span class="cm"># ── Greedy / Beam search ─────────────────────────────────</span>\n    do_sample=<span class="kw">False</span>,         <span class="cm"># if False: greedy or beam search (deterministic)</span>\n    num_beams=<span class="num">4</span>,             <span class="cm"># beam width; 1 = greedy, >1 = beam search</span>\n    early_stopping=<span class="kw">True</span>,     <span class="cm"># stop beams once eos found (only with num_beams>1)</span>\n    no_repeat_ngram_size=<span class="num">3</span>,  <span class="cm"># forbid repeating any 3-gram</span>\n    length_penalty=<span class="num">1.0</span>,      <span class="cm"># <1 favors shorter; >1 favors longer (beam only)</span>\n\n    <span class="cm"># ── Stochastic sampling (do_sample=True) ─────────────────</span>\n    temperature=<span class="num">0.8</span>,         <span class="cm"># <1 = sharper (less random); >1 = flatter (more random)</span>\n    top_k=<span class="num">50</span>,                <span class="cm"># keep only the 50 highest-probability next tokens</span>\n    top_p=<span class="num">0.92</span>,              <span class="cm"># nucleus: keep smallest set with cumulative prob >= 0.92</span>\n    repetition_penalty=<span class="num">1.2</span>,  <span class="cm"># penalize tokens already in the sequence</span>\n\n    <span class="cm"># ── Multiple outputs ─────────────────────────────────────</span>\n    num_return_sequences=<span class="num">3</span>,  <span class="cm"># generate 3 independent continuations</span>\n\n    <span class="cm"># ── Reproducibility ──────────────────────────────────────</span>\n    <span class="cm"># Set torch.manual_seed(42) before generate() for reproducible sampling</span>\n)</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How to read this generate() reference call:</strong> 1) <strong>Length:</strong> <code>max_new_tokens=150</code> caps only the newly generated tokens (preferred over <code>max_length</code>), and <code>min_new_tokens=30</code> prevents trivially short outputs. 2) <strong>Stopping:</strong> <code>eos_token_id</code> and <code>pad_token_id</code> tell the decoder when to stop and how to handle batched padding. 3) <strong>Beam search:</strong> <code>do_sample=False</code> with <code>num_beams=4</code>, <code>early_stopping=True</code>, <code>no_repeat_ngram_size=3</code> and <code>length_penalty=1.0</code> runs deterministic beam search. 4) <strong>Sampling:</strong> setting <code>do_sample=True</code> activates <code>temperature=0.8</code>, <code>top_k=50</code>, <code>top_p=0.92</code> and <code>repetition_penalty=1.2</code> for nucleus sampling with a soft repetition guard. 5) <code>num_return_sequences=3</code> produces three independent continuations in a single call.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="calc-card-title">max_new_tokens</div><div class="calc-card-body">Prefer this over <code>max_length</code>. It caps only the <em>new</em> tokens generated, regardless of prompt length. With <code>max_length=200</code> and a 180-token prompt you get only 20 new tokens; with <code>max_new_tokens=200</code> you always get 200 new ones.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="calc-card-title">temperature</div><div class="calc-card-body">Divides all logits by T before softmax. T=1.0 is the model\'s raw distribution. T&lt;1 concentrates mass on high-probability tokens (safe, repetitive). T&gt;1 flattens the distribution (diverse, unpredictable). T=0 approaches greedy argmax. Only active when <code>do_sample=True</code>.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="calc-card-title">top_k</div><div class="calc-card-body">Filters the vocabulary to the k highest-probability tokens before sampling. Prevents sampling from the long tail of improbable tokens. k=50 is a robust default. Very low k (5\u201310) = focused; very high k (500+) = diverse but risky. Only active when <code>do_sample=True</code>.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="calc-card-title">top_p (nucleus)</div><div class="calc-card-body">Dynamically selects the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability \u2265 p, then samples from that set. Unlike top_k, the set size adapts to the model\'s confidence. When the model is certain, nucleus is small (focused); when uncertain, it is large (diverse). p=0.9\u20130.95 is the standard range.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="calc-card-title">num_beams</div><div class="calc-card-body">Beam search maintains B candidate sequences simultaneously, expanding the most probable at each step. It is deterministic and tends to produce fluent, coherent text but can be repetitive. num_beams=4\u20136 is typical for summarization; num_beams=1 is pure greedy. Incompatible with <code>do_sample=True</code> (use <code>num_beam_groups</code> + <code>diversity_penalty</code> for diverse beams).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="calc-card-title">repetition_penalty</div><div class="calc-card-body">Before sampling, divides the logit of any token that has already appeared in the sequence by this value (if logit &gt;0) or multiplies it (if logit &lt;0). Values 1.1\u20131.3 noticeably reduce repetition. Works independently of <code>no_repeat_ngram_size</code> and can be combined with it for stronger deduplication.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

/* ── Temperature Comparison ───────────────────────────────── */
