/* ── Decoding Strategy Comparison ────────────────────────────*/
+ '<h4 class="l-subsection-title">Decoding Strategy Comparison</h4>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="ccomp-col"><div class="ccomp-title" style="color:#4ecdc4;">Greedy Decoding</div><div class="ccomp-body">'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Algorithm:</strong> At each step, pick the single highest-probability next token. Repeat until max length or EOS.</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Pros:</strong> Fast (one forward pass per step), deterministic, no hyperparameters to tune.</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Cons:</strong> Locally optimal choices compound into globally suboptimal sequences. Notoriously repetitive. Misses sequences with high overall probability but low individual step probability.</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0;"><strong>When to use:</strong> Baselines, short outputs, when speed matters more than quality.</p>'
+ '</div></div>'
+ '<div class="ccomp-col"><div class="ccomp-title" style="color:#c8a96e;">Beam Search</div><div class="ccomp-body">'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Algorithm:</strong> Maintain B candidate sequences. At each step expand each beam to its top-B successors, keep the B highest-scoring overall sequences. Score = sum of log-probs / length_penalty.</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Pros:</strong> Finds sequences with higher joint probability than greedy. Excellent for translation and summarization. Fluent output.</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Cons:</strong> B\u00d7 more compute and memory. Still deterministic (no diversity). Can be generic / boring for open-ended generation.</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0;"><strong>When to use:</strong> Summarization, translation, structured text. num_beams=4 or 5 is the standard default.</p>'
+ '</div></div>'
+ '<div class="ccomp-col"><div class="ccomp-title" style="color:#ff4b4b;">Sampling</div><div class="ccomp-body">'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Algorithm:</strong> At each step, sample the next token from the full (temperature-scaled) probability distribution over the vocabulary.</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Pros:</strong> Diverse, creative outputs. Can be combined with top_k and top_p for principled variance control. Never gets stuck in repetitive loops (with repetition_penalty).</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Cons:</strong> Non-deterministic. High variance. Can produce incoherent text at high temperatures.</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0;"><strong>When to use:</strong> Creative writing, story generation, chatbots, any open-ended task where diversity is valued.</p>'
+ '</div></div>'
+ '<div class="ccomp-col"><div class="ccomp-title" style="color:#a0e0b0;">Nucleus (top_p)</div><div class="ccomp-body">'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Algorithm:</strong> At each step, take the smallest token set whose cumulative probability \u2265 p. Renormalize and sample from that set only. Nucleus size adapts dynamically to model confidence.</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Pros:</strong> Best of sampling without the long-tail risk. When model is confident, keeps choices tight; when uncertain, opens up. State-of-the-art for open-ended generation quality.</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0 0 8px 0;"><strong>Cons:</strong> One more hyperparameter (p). Slightly more complex than top_k.</p>'
+ '<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#ccc;margin:0;"><strong>When to use:</strong> Default choice for high-quality open-ended generation. Combine with temperature=0.8\u20131.0.</p>'
+ '</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 2: Fine-Tuning GPT-2 for Custom Text Generation
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">Fine-Tuning GPT-2 for Custom Text Generation</h3>'

+ '<p class="l-text">The base GPT-2 has learned general English from the web. Fine-tuning adapts it to a specific domain, style, or topic. A GPT-2 fine-tuned on medical literature generates medically accurate text; one fine-tuned on Python code generates Python; one fine-tuned on a specific author\u2019s essays mimics their style. The process requires only a plain text corpus and a few hours of GPU time.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text">For causal language modeling there are no input/output label pairs. The model learns to predict every token given its left context \u2014 the same objective as pre-training. The <code>DataCollatorForLanguageModeling</code> handles the bookkeeping of shifting inputs and labels by one position.</p>'

+ '<h4 class="l-subsection-title">DataCollatorForLanguageModeling</h4>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON (DEMO)</span> DataCollator for Language Modeling <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> datasets <span class="kw">import</span> load_dataset\n<span class="kw">from</span> transformers <span class="kw">import</span> (\n    AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM,\n    DataCollatorForLanguageModeling,\n    Trainer, TrainingArguments\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># ── 1. Load and tokenize your corpus ─────────────────────────</span>\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"gpt2"</span>)\ntokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token\n\n<span class="cm"># Example: loading a text dataset from a local file</span>\n<span class="cm"># Each row has a "text" field</span>\ndataset = <span class="fn">load_dataset</span>(<span class="str">"text"</span>, data_files={<span class="str">"train"</span>: <span class="str">"corpus.txt"</span>})\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">tokenize_fn</span>(examples):\n    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">tokenizer</span>(\n        examples[<span class="str">"text"</span>],\n        truncation=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n        max_length=<span class="num">512</span>,\n        <span class="cm"># No padding here — the collator handles it dynamically</span>\n    )\n\ntokenized = dataset.<span class="fn">map</span>(\n    tokenize_fn,\n    batched=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n    remove_columns=[<span class="str">"text"</span>],  <span class="cm"># drop the raw text column</span>\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># ── 2. DataCollatorForLanguageModeling ───────────────────────</span>\n<span class="cm"># mlm=False  → causal language modeling (GPT-2 style)</span>\n<span class="cm"># mlm=True   → masked language modeling (BERT style, adds random masks)</span>\ncollator = <span class="fn">DataCollatorForLanguageModeling</span>(\n    tokenizer=tokenizer,\n    mlm=<span class="kw">False</span>,          <span class="cm"># CRITICAL: False for GPT-2</span>\n    <span class="cm"># mlm_probability=0.15  # only relevant when mlm=True</span>\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># What the collator does internally:</span>\n<span class="cm"># - Pads sequences to the batch maximum length</span>\n<span class="cm"># - Sets labels = input_ids (shifted right inside the model)</span>\n<span class="cm"># - Sets labels[padding_positions] = -100 (ignored in loss)</span></code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How the LM dataset and collator are wired:</strong> 1) <code>AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("gpt2")</code> and <code>tokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token</code> prepare the BPE tokenizer (GPT-2 has no native pad token). 2) <code>load_dataset("text", data_files={"train": "corpus.txt"})</code> loads a plain-text corpus where each row exposes a <code>text</code> field. 3) <code>tokenize_fn(examples)</code> truncates to 512 tokens without padding (the collator pads dynamically), and <code>dataset.map(tokenize_fn, batched=True, remove_columns=["text"])</code> tokenizes the entire corpus in parallel and drops the raw text. 4) <code>DataCollatorForLanguageModeling(tokenizer=tokenizer, mlm=False)</code> creates the causal-LM collator — <code>mlm=False</code> is the critical flag for GPT-2; setting it <code>True</code> would activate BERT-style random masking instead.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Why labels = input_ids?</strong> In causal LM, the model predicts token t+1 from all tokens up to t. Setting labels equal to input_ids means: at position 0, predict input_ids[1]; at position 1, predict input_ids[2]; and so on. The model internally shifts labels right by one. Padding positions receive label -100, which PyTorch\'s <code>CrossEntropyLoss</code> ignores by convention, so no gradient flows through pad tokens.</div>'

+ '<h4 class="l-subsection-title">Training with Trainer</h4>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span> Trainer Fine-Tuning Setup <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code><span class="cm"># ── 3. Load model ────────────────────────────────────────────</span>\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"gpt2"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ── 4. Training arguments ────────────────────────────────────</span>\ntraining_args = <span class="fn">TrainingArguments</span>(\n    output_dir=<span class="str">"./gpt2-finetuned"</span>,\n    overwrite_output_dir=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n\n    num_train_epochs=<span class="num">3</span>,\n    per_device_train_batch_size=<span class="num">4</span>,     <span class="cm"># reduce if OOM</span>\n    gradient_accumulation_steps=<span class="num">8</span>,    <span class="cm"># effective batch = 4*8 = 32</span>\n\n    learning_rate=<span class="num">5e-5</span>,               <span class="cm"># lower than BERT fine-tuning</span>\n    lr_scheduler_type=<span class="str">"cosine"</span>,       <span class="cm"># cosine decay from peak LR</span>\n    warmup_ratio=<span class="num">0.1</span>,                 <span class="cm"># 10% of steps for linear warmup</span>\n    weight_decay=<span class="num">0.01</span>,\n\n    fp16=<span class="kw">True</span>,                        <span class="cm"># half-precision training (GPU)</span>\n    <span class="cm"># bf16=True,                      # alternative on Ampere+ GPUs</span>\n\n    logging_steps=<span class="num">50</span>,\n    save_steps=<span class="num">500</span>,\n    eval_strategy=<span class="str">"steps"</span>,            <span class="cm"># transformers 4.46+ (önceki: evaluation_strategy)</span>\n    eval_steps=<span class="num">500</span>,\n    load_best_model_at_end=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n    metric_for_best_model=<span class="str">"eval_loss"</span>,\n\n    report_to=<span class="str">"none"</span>,\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># ── 5. Trainer ───────────────────────────────────────────────</span>\ntrainer = <span class="fn">Trainer</span>(\n    model=model,\n    args=training_args,\n    train_dataset=tokenized[<span class="str">"train"</span>],\n    <span class="cm"># eval_dataset=tokenized["validation"],  # if you have a val split</span>\n    data_collator=collator,\n    tokenizer=tokenizer,\n)\n\ntrainer.<span class="fn">train</span>()\ntrainer.<span class="fn">save_model</span>(<span class="str">"./gpt2-finetuned"</span>)\ntokenizer.<span class="fn">save_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"./gpt2-finetuned"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Fine-tuning complete."</span>)</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How this Trainer fine-tunes GPT-2:</strong> 1) <code>AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained("gpt2")</code> loads the base causal-LM head and weights. 2) <code>TrainingArguments(output_dir="./gpt2-finetuned", num_train_epochs=3, per_device_train_batch_size=4, gradient_accumulation_steps=8, learning_rate=5e-5, lr_scheduler_type="cosine", warmup_ratio=0.1, weight_decay=0.01, fp16=True, ...)</code> defines a 3-epoch run with an effective batch of 32 and cosine decay — the recipe most published GPT-2 fine-tunes use. 3) <code>Trainer(model=model, args=training_args, train_dataset=tokenized["train"], data_collator=collator, tokenizer=tokenizer)</code> assembles the loop using the LM collator from the previous block. 4) <code>trainer.train()</code> runs the optimization and <code>trainer.save_model("./gpt2-finetuned")</code> + <code>tokenizer.save_pretrained("./gpt2-finetuned")</code> persist the final checkpoint so the next snippet can reload it.</p>'

+ '<h4 class="l-subsection-title">Generating from the Fine-Tuned Model</h4>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON (DEMO)</span> Generate from Fine-Tuned Model <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> transformers <span class="kw">import</span> pipeline\n\n<span class="cm"># Load the fine-tuned model using the pipeline interface</span>\ngenerator = <span class="fn">pipeline</span>(\n    <span class="str">"text-generation"</span>,\n    model=<span class="str">"./gpt2-finetuned"</span>,\n    tokenizer=<span class="str">"./gpt2-finetuned"</span>,\n    device=<span class="num">0</span> <span class="kw">if</span> torch.cuda.<span class="fn">is_available</span>() <span class="kw">else</span> -<span class="num">1</span>,\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># Generate with nucleus sampling — recommended for fine-tuned models</span>\nresults = <span class="fn">generator</span>(\n    <span class="str">"The study found that"</span>,\n    max_new_tokens=<span class="num">120</span>,\n    do_sample=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n    temperature=<span class="num">0.8</span>,\n    top_p=<span class="num">0.92</span>,\n    repetition_penalty=<span class="num">1.2</span>,\n    no_repeat_ngram_size=<span class="num">3</span>,\n    num_return_sequences=<span class="num">3</span>,\n)\n\n<span class="kw">for</span> i, r <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">enumerate</span>(results):\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"\</span>\n--- Output {i+<span class="num">1</span>} ---<span class="str">")</span>\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(r[<span class="str">"generated_text"</span>])\n\n<span class="cm"># Or use the model directly for more control</span>\n<span class="kw">from</span> transformers <span class="kw">import</span> AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"./gpt2-finetuned"</span>)\nmodel     = AutoModelForCausalLM.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"./gpt2-finetuned"</span>)\n\nprompt  = <span class="str">"The study found that"</span>\ninputs  = <span class="fn">tokenizer</span>(prompt, return_tensors=<span class="str">"pt"</span>)\nout_ids = model.<span class="fn">generate</span>(\n    **inputs,\n    max_new_tokens=<span class="num">120</span>,\n    do_sample=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n    temperature=<span class="num">0.8</span>,\n    top_p=<span class="num">0.92</span>,\n    repetition_penalty=<span class="num">1.2</span>,\n    no_repeat_ngram_size=<span class="num">3</span>,\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(tokenizer.<span class="fn">decode</span>(out_ids[<span class="num">0</span>], skip_special_tokens=<span class="kw">True</span>))</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Two ways to generate from the fine-tuned model:</strong> 1) <strong>Pipeline path:</strong> <code>pipeline("text-generation", model="./gpt2-finetuned", tokenizer="./gpt2-finetuned", device=0 if torch.cuda.is_available() else -1)</code> wraps the saved checkpoint into a single callable, then <code>generator("The study found that", max_new_tokens=120, do_sample=True, temperature=0.8, top_p=0.92, repetition_penalty=1.2, no_repeat_ngram_size=3, num_return_sequences=3)</code> returns three nucleus-sampled continuations. 2) <strong>Manual path:</strong> reloading via <code>AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("./gpt2-finetuned")</code> + <code>AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained("./gpt2-finetuned")</code> gives you raw <code>model.generate(...)</code> access and full control over the decoded tensor — useful when you need raw logits or custom post-processing.</p>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: Text Summarization with T5
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">Text Summarization with T5</h3>'

+ '<p class="l-text">T5 (Text-To-Text Transfer Transformer) reframes every NLP task as a sequence-to-sequence problem. Instead of task-specific output heads (a classification head for sentiment, a span extractor for QA), T5 always maps a text string to another text string. Translation becomes "translate English to German: [source]". Summarization becomes "summarize: [source]". Sentiment becomes "sentiment: [text]" \u2192 "positive". This unified format means a single model architecture and a single training objective cover the entire NLP task space.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>The T5 "text-to-text" format:</strong> Every task is expressed as a prefix + input text \u2192 output text. For summarization the prefix is literally <code>summarize: </code> followed by the document. The model was pre-trained to associate this prefix with generating condensed, abstractive summaries. At inference and fine-tuning time, including this prefix is <em>mandatory</em> \u2014 omitting it degrades output quality because the model does not know which task to perform. This is the most common mistake when using T5 for the first time.</div>'

+ '<h4 class="l-subsection-title">Loading T5 and BART</h4>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON (DEMO)</span> Load T5 &amp; BART Models <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> transformers <span class="kw">import</span> AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM\n\n<span class="cm"># ── T5 variants ──────────────────────────────────────────────</span>\n<span class="cm"># t5-small   (60M params)  — fits on CPU, good for experiments</span>\n<span class="cm"># t5-base    (220M params) — best quality/speed tradeoff</span>\n<span class="cm"># t5-large   (770M params) — production quality</span>\n<span class="cm"># google/flan-t5-base      — instruction-tuned T5, stronger zero-shot</span>\n\nt5_tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"t5-base"</span>)\nt5_model     = AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"t5-base"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ── BART variants ────────────────────────────────────────────</span>\n<span class="cm"># facebook/bart-base        (139M) — base BART</span>\n<span class="cm"># facebook/bart-large-cnn   (406M) — fine-tuned on CNN/DailyMail</span>\n<span class="cm">#                                     → best out-of-the-box summarizer</span>\n<span class="cm"># facebook/bart-large-xsum  (406M) — fine-tuned on XSum (shorter, punchier)</span>\n\nbart_tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"facebook/bart-large-cnn"</span>)\nbart_model     = AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"facebook/bart-large-cnn"</span>)\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"T5 params:   {t5_model.num_parameters():,}"</span>)    <span class="cm"># 222,903,552</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"BART params: {bart_model.num_parameters():,}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># 406,291,456</span></code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Loading T5 and BART side by side:</strong> 1) <code>AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("t5-base")</code> + <code>AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.from_pretrained("t5-base")</code> brings up the 220 M-parameter encoder-decoder; the inline comment lists the other T5 sizes (<code>t5-small</code>, <code>t5-large</code>) and the instruction-tuned <code>google/flan-t5-base</code> variant. 2) <code>AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("facebook/bart-large-cnn")</code> + <code>AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.from_pretrained("facebook/bart-large-cnn")</code> loads the 406 M-parameter BART already fine-tuned on CNN/DailyMail — the strongest out-of-the-box summarizer. 3) The parameter prints (<code>222,903,552</code> vs <code>406,291,456</code>) confirm both models are loaded correctly and ready for inference or further fine-tuning.</p>'

+ '<h4 class="l-subsection-title">Summarization Pipeline</h4>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON (DEMO)</span> T5 &amp; BART Summarization Pipeline <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> transformers <span class="kw">import</span> pipeline\n\n<span class="cm"># ── Quick T5 summarization ────────────────────────────────────</span>\nt5_summarizer = <span class="fn">pipeline</span>(\n    <span class="str">"summarization"</span>,\n    model=<span class="str">"t5-base"</span>,\n    tokenizer=<span class="str">"t5-base"</span>,\n    device=<span class="num">0</span> <span class="kw">if</span> torch.cuda.<span class="fn">is_available</span>() <span class="kw">else</span> -<span class="num">1</span>,\n)\n\ndocument = <span class="str">"""\nDeep learning has transformed computer vision, natural language processing,\nand speech recognition over the past decade. Neural networks with many layers\ncan now match or exceed human performance on a range of perceptual tasks.\nHowever, these models require enormous amounts of labeled training data and\ncomputational resources, raising concerns about accessibility and environmental\nimpact. Researchers are actively exploring methods to reduce these requirements\nthrough techniques such as transfer learning, data augmentation, and efficient\narchitecture design.\n"""</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># For t5-base, the pipeline automatically prepends "summarize: "</span>\nsummary = <span class="fn">t5_summarizer</span>(\n    document,\n    max_length=<span class="num">80</span>,\n    min_length=<span class="num">20</span>,\n    do_sample=<span class="kw">False</span>,   <span class="cm"># beam search for summarization</span>\n    num_beams=<span class="num">4</span>,\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(summary[<span class="num">0</span>][<span class="str">"summary_text"</span>])\n\n<span class="cm"># ── Manual T5 summarization (full control) ────────────────────</span>\ninput_text = <span class="str">"summarize: "</span> + document.<span class="fn">strip</span>()  <span class="cm"># prepend prefix manually</span>\ninputs     = <span class="fn">t5_tokenizer</span>(\n    input_text,\n    return_tensors=<span class="str">"pt"</span>,\n    truncation=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n    max_length=<span class="num">512</span>,\n)\n\nsummary_ids = t5_model.<span class="fn">generate</span>(\n    **inputs,\n    max_new_tokens=<span class="num">80</span>,\n    min_length=<span class="num">20</span>,\n    num_beams=<span class="num">4</span>,\n    length_penalty=<span class="num">2.0</span>,   <span class="cm"># >1 encourages longer summaries</span>\n    early_stopping=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n    no_repeat_ngram_size=<span class="num">3</span>,\n)\n\nresult = t5_tokenizer.<span class="fn">decode</span>(summary_ids[<span class="num">0</span>], skip_special_tokens=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(result)\n\n<span class="cm"># ── BART summarization (no prefix needed) ────────────────────</span>\nbart_summarizer = <span class="fn">pipeline</span>(\n    <span class="str">"summarization"</span>,\n    model=<span class="str">"facebook/bart-large-cnn"</span>,\n    device=<span class="num">0</span> <span class="kw">if</span> torch.cuda.<span class="fn">is_available</span>() <span class="kw">else</span> -<span class="num">1</span>,\n)\n\nbart_summary = <span class="fn">bart_summarizer</span>(\n    document,\n    max_length=<span class="num">80</span>,\n    min_length=<span class="num">20</span>,\n    do_sample=<span class="kw">False</span>,\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(bart_summary[<span class="num">0</span>][<span class="str">"summary_text"</span>])</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Three summarization paths in this snippet:</strong> 1) <strong>T5 pipeline:</strong> <code>pipeline("summarization", model="t5-base", tokenizer="t5-base", device=...)</code> automatically prepends <code>"summarize: "</code> behind the scenes and runs beam search with <code>max_length=80, min_length=20, num_beams=4</code>. 2) <strong>Manual T5:</strong> manually prepending <code>"summarize: "</code> to the document, tokenizing with <code>max_length=512</code> and calling <code>t5_model.generate(..., num_beams=4, length_penalty=2.0, early_stopping=True, no_repeat_ngram_size=3)</code> gives full control over the decoding hyperparameters. 3) <strong>BART pipeline:</strong> <code>pipeline("summarization", model="facebook/bart-large-cnn", ...)</code> needs no task prefix — BART\'s denoising pre-training makes raw text-in, raw text-out the natural interface.</p>'

/* Fine-tuning T5 */
+ '<h4 class="l-subsection-title">Fine-Tuning T5 for Custom Summarization</h4>'

+ '<p class="l-text">When your target domain differs from the CNN/DailyMail news articles T5 and BART were pre-trained on \u2014 for example, scientific abstracts, legal documents, or patient records \u2014 fine-tuning on domain-specific data significantly improves summary quality. The fine-tuning process requires (document, summary) pairs and uses <code>Seq2SeqTrainer</code>.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON (DEMO)</span> Fine-Tune T5 Dataset &amp; Collator <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> datasets <span class="kw">import</span> load_dataset\n<span class="kw">from</span> transformers <span class="kw">import</span> (\n    AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM,\n    DataCollatorForSeq2Seq,\n    Seq2SeqTrainer, Seq2SeqTrainingArguments,\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># ── 1. Dataset ────────────────────────────────────────────────</span>\n<span class="cm"># Using the CNN/DailyMail dataset as an example</span>\n<span class="cm"># Replace with your own domain dataset for custom fine-tuning</span>\ndataset    = <span class="fn">load_dataset</span>(<span class="str">"cnn_dailymail"</span>, <span class="str">"3.0.0"</span>)\ntokenizer  = AutoTokenizer.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"t5-base"</span>)\n\nMAX_INPUT  = <span class="num">512</span>   <span class="cm"># max document length in tokens</span>\nMAX_TARGET = <span class="num">128</span>   <span class="cm"># max summary length in tokens</span>\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">preprocess</span>(examples):\n    <span class="cm"># T5 requires the "summarize: " prefix</span>\n    inputs  = [<span class="str">"summarize: "</span> + doc <span class="kw">for</span> doc <span class="kw">in</span> examples[<span class="str">"article"</span>]]\n    targets = examples[<span class="str">"highlights"</span>]  <span class="cm"># gold summaries</span>\n\n    <span class="cm"># Tokenize inputs</span>\n    model_inputs = <span class="fn">tokenizer</span>(\n        inputs,\n        max_length=MAX_INPUT,\n        truncation=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n        padding=<span class="kw">False</span>,   <span class="cm"># dynamic padding via collator</span>\n    )\n\n    <span class="cm"># Tokenize targets (use tokenizer\'s as_target_tokenizer context</span>\n    <span class="cm"># for T5 — it sets the eos token correctly)</span>\n    <span class="kw">with</span> tokenizer.<span class="fn">as_target_tokenizer</span>():\n        labels = <span class="fn">tokenizer</span>(\n            targets,\n            max_length=MAX_TARGET,\n            truncation=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n            padding=<span class="kw">False</span>,\n        )\n\n    model_inputs[<span class="str">"labels"</span>] = labels[<span class="str">"input_ids"</span>]\n    <span class="kw">return</span> model_inputs\n\ntokenized_ds = dataset.<span class="fn">map</span>(\n    preprocess,\n    batched=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n    remove_columns=dataset[<span class="str">"train"</span>].column_names,\n)\n\n<span class="cm"># ── 2. Data collator ──────────────────────────────────────────</span>\n<span class="cm"># DataCollatorForSeq2Seq handles padding for both inputs and labels</span>\n<span class="cm"># and replaces label padding token IDs with -100</span>\nmodel    = AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"t5-base"</span>)\ncollator = <span class="fn">DataCollatorForSeq2Seq</span>(\n    tokenizer=tokenizer,\n    model=model,       <span class="cm"># needed to determine decoder_start_token_id</span>\n    padding=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n    label_pad_token_id=-<span class="num">100</span>,\n)</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How the T5 fine-tuning dataset is prepared:</strong> 1) <code>load_dataset("cnn_dailymail", "3.0.0")</code> brings down the canonical news-summarization corpus with parallel <code>article</code> / <code>highlights</code> columns. 2) <code>preprocess(examples)</code> prepends the mandatory <code>"summarize: "</code> prefix to every article, then tokenizes inputs with <code>max_length=512</code> and targets with <code>max_length=128</code>. The <code>tokenizer.as_target_tokenizer()</code> context manager ensures the target side uses the correct EOS handling. 3) Assigning <code>model_inputs["labels"] = labels["input_ids"]</code> turns the (article, summary) pair into a proper seq2seq training example. 4) <code>DataCollatorForSeq2Seq(tokenizer=tokenizer, model=model, padding=True, label_pad_token_id=-100)</code> pads both inputs and labels dynamically per batch and uses <code>-100</code> so padded label positions are ignored in the loss.</p>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 4: BART for Summarization
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">BART for Summarization</h3>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>BART = Bidirectional and Auto-Regressive Transformer.</strong> BART is a denoising autoencoder: during pre-training, the input document is corrupted by five types of noise (token masking, deletion, text infilling, sentence permutation, and document rotation), and the decoder must reconstruct the original uncorrupted document. This pre-training objective is a superset of both BERT (bidirectional encoder) and GPT (autoregressive decoder), which is why BART excels at generation tasks like summarization, translation, and dialogue. For summarization specifically, sentence permutation + text infilling teach the model to reorder and condense information \u2014 exactly what abstractive summarization requires.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">BART\'s architecture is a standard transformer encoder-decoder. The encoder is bidirectional (full self-attention over the entire input). The decoder is autoregressive (causal self-attention + cross-attention over encoder outputs). At fine-tuning time for summarization, the source document feeds the encoder, and the target summary is generated by the decoder token-by-token. The denoising pre-training transfers remarkably well: <code>facebook/bart-large-cnn</code> achieves state-of-the-art ROUGE scores on CNN/DailyMail without any further fine-tuning.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="ccomp-col"><div class="ccomp-title" style="color:#c8a96e;">T5</div><div class="ccomp-body">'
+ '<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:0.85rem;">'
+ '<tr><td style="padding:5px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid #333;color:#aaa;">Architecture</td><td style="padding:5px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid #333;color:#e0e0e0;">Encoder-decoder transformer (standard)</td></tr>'
+ '<tr><td style="padding:5px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid #333;color:#aaa;">Pre-training</td><td style="padding:5px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid #333;color:#e0e0e0;">Span prediction on C4 (750 GB) with masked token prediction</td></tr>'
+ '<tr><td style="padding:5px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid #333;color:#aaa;">Task format</td><td style="padding:5px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid #333;color:#e0e0e0;">Text-to-text with task prefix ("summarize: ", "translate: ")</td></tr>'
+ '<tr><td style="padding:5px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid #333;color:#aaa;">Tokenizer</td><td style="padding:5px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid #333;color:#e0e0e0;">SentencePiece Unigram, 32 000-token vocab</td></tr>'
+ '<tr><td style="padding:5px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid #333;color:#aaa;">Best for</td><td style="padding:5px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid #333;color:#e0e0e0;">Multi-task fine-tuning, instruction-following (Flan-T5), long-form generation</td></tr>'
+ '<tr><td style="padding:5px 6px;color:#aaa;">ROUGE (CNN/DM)</td><td style="padding:5px 6px;color:#e0e0e0;">R-1: 42.5, R-2: 20.9, R-L: 39.4 (t5-large, zero-shot)</td></tr>'
